<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Esatto.CookieScan.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <!--
      The command name, deliberately the same in both shapes this project ships as: `dotnet tool
      install` exposes ToolCommandName, and a self-contained publish produces AssemblyName.exe. An
      operator who read one set of docs must not find the other spelling on disk.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>esatto-cookiescan</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      NU5111, eight times, over Playwright's own .ps1 files under .playwright\package\bin\. The
      warning is about packages.config-era install hooks: NuGet runs install.ps1 / init.ps1 from the
      root of tools\, and warns about any other script there in case one was meant to be a hook that
      was misnamed. These are Playwright's browser-install helpers, they sit nested several folders
      deep, and a DotnetTool package executes nothing at install time regardless. Suppressed rather
      than worked around - the alternative is excluding content the driver needs.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5111</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="dotnet tool">
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>esatto-cookiescan</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>Esatto.CookieScan.Cli</PackageId>
    <Authors>Carl Schéle</Authors>
    <Description>A .NET tool that audits a site's cookie consent banner. Drives a real headless browser through the site once per consent decision a visitor can make, and compares what actually gets set against what the cookie policy page declares. Anything set outside the consent it needed is a violation; anything undeclared can be appended to the policy page as a draft for an editor to review. Install with `dotnet tool install -g Esatto.CookieScan.Cli`, then run `esatto-cookiescan --url https://example.com`.</Description>
    <PackageTags>dotnet-tool;cookie-consent;gdpr;eprivacy;cookie-scanner;cookie-audit;umbraco</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/carl-schele-esatto/Esatto.Packages/tree/main/Esatto.CookieScan.Cli</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/carl-schele-esatto/Esatto.Packages</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The single-file switches apply only to a RID-specific publish, and must NOT apply when packing
    the tool. A tool package is framework-dependent and is launched by a shim that resolves the
    managed dll beside its own deps.json; bundling that dll into a self-extracting host leaves the
    shim with nothing to resolve. `dotnet pack` sets no RuntimeIdentifier, a self-contained publish
    always does, so the RID is the honest discriminator between the two shapes.

    Why they are needed at all for the exe shape: Playwright ships native libraries that a
    single-file bundle cannot load from memory, hence IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract. That is
    not sufficient on its own - Playwright's browser driver is a whole Node.js runtime plus its own
    JS files under .playwright\, which the SDK classifies as content rather than as a native
    library, so it was left behind as loose files beside the exe and a copy-anywhere run failed at
    "Checking for a Chromium build..." with "Microsoft.Playwright assembly was found, but is missing
    required assets." IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract bundles that content too and extracts it on
    first run.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
    <IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract>true</IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Esatto.CookieScan.Engine\Esatto.CookieScan.Engine.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
